<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">García Ramírez of Navarre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1112–1150</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as García the Restorer, García Ramírez the Restorer · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimenez-dynasty">Jiménez dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">García Ramírez, called the Restorer, was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> from 1134 to 1150. On the death of Alfonso the Battler, who had ruled both Aragon and Navarre, the Navarrese nobles chose him as king, restoring Navarre's independence. He became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vii-of-leon-and-castile">Alfonso VII of Castile</a> and joined his campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a> in 1147.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/garcia-ramirez-of-navarre.jpg" alt="García Ramírez of Navarre" width="317" height="568" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Garcia_(IV)_Ramires_de_Navarra_-_Compendio_de_cr%C3%B3nicas_de_reyes_(Biblioteca_Nacional_de_Espa%C3%B1a).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>García Ramírez was born around 1112, the son of Ramiro Sánchez, lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monzon">Monzón</a>, whose father Sancho was an illegitimate son of King García Sánchez III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>. His mother Cristina was a daughter of the Castilian nobleman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">Rodrigo Díaz de Vivar</a>, known as El Cid. He succeeded his father as lord of Monzón and also held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/logrono">Logroño</a>. After the murder of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iv-of-navarre">Sancho IV of Navarre</a> in 1076, Navarre had been divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and Aragon, and Aragon's kings claimed the Navarrese crown, so that for 58 years the two kingdoms were united.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When the childless Alfonso the Battler died in 1134, the succession to both kingdoms was disputed. His will left them to three crusading orders, which the nobility of Aragon rejected in favour of his brother Ramiro, a monk. The Navarrese were doubtful of Ramiro and wary of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vii-of-leon-and-castile">Alfonso VII of León and Castile</a>, and at first preferred Pedro de Atarés.</p><p>At an assembly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> they turned away from Pedro, alienated by his arrogance, and elected García Ramírez, a descendant of their own dynasty. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Pamplona gave him his church's treasure to fund his government. In January 1135 the Pact of Vadoluengo made García the adopted son of Ramiro, and in May he declared himself a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Alfonso VII, who recognised his royal status.</p><p>Alfonso gave him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> before September 1135, but by 1137 García's rule there had closed. In 1136 he had to give Rioja to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, and in 1137 he allied with Alfonso I of Portugal against Alfonso VII before peace was confirmed in 1139 or 1140. In 1144 he married Urraca, an illegitimate daughter of Alfonso VII, to strengthen the tie to his overlord. He took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a> and was instrumental in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a> in 1147, and in 1146 he occupied Tauste, held by Aragon, until Alfonso VII mediated peace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>García's first wife, Margaret of L'Aigle, bore his successor Sancho VI and two daughters who married kings: Blanche, who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iii-of-castile">Sancho III of Castile</a>, and Margaret, who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-i-of-sicily">William I of Sicily</a>. He refused to acknowledge her son Rodrigo as his own. García died on 21 November 1150 and was buried in the cathedral of Santa María la Real in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>. His election restored the independence of the kingdom, which earned him the name the Restorer. His main monument is the Romanesque monastery of Santa María de la Oliva at Carcastillo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “García Ramírez of Navarre” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1147 Alfonso VII of Castile, styled Emperor of Spain, led a coalition including Genoa, Pisa, Ramon Berenguer IV of Barcelona and <b>García Ramírez of Navarre</b> against Almería, then a corsair base.</blockquote><a href="/state/castilian-almeria">Castilian Almería</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Lorka/Lorca, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.67,-1.95&amp;year=1136" aria-label="Lorka/Lorca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4503 647 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of García Ramírez of Navarre"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4953 917h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4953 917h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=42.67,-1.95&amp;year=1136">What was here in 1136? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
